Datiscum Linux 6.18.40
======================

This branch is based on the official Linux v6.18.40 tag and carries the
kernel changes used by the datiscum systems.  The changes are split into
separate Git commits so that every patch group can be audited or replaced by
its stable upstream equivalent in later kernel releases.

Scheduler stability
-------------------

The scheduler series contains the upstream fair-scheduler overflow fixes:

* 4823725d9d1d ("sched/fair: Increase weight bits for avg_vruntime")
* 556146ce5e94 ("sched/fair: Avoid overflow in enqueue_entity()")
* 416dd8fc352b ("sched/fair: Fix overflow in vruntime_eligible()")

It also contains the datiscum-specific defensive check in avg_vruntime():

	if (unlikely(weight <= 0))
		delta = curr ? entity_key(cfs_rq, curr) : 0;

The fallback prevents a zero or negative divisor after inconsistent or
wrapped weight accounting.  It deliberately continues through
update_zero_vruntime(), avoiding both a divide-by-zero panic and an invalid
vruntime jump.

This fixes the scheduler failure path observed before these patches.  That
failure could result in a kernel panic, soft or hard lockups, RCU stalls, or
tasks no longer receiving CPU time.  The fix is particularly relevant to
heavily parallel hosts with many CPU cores, where scheduler failures have a
large system-wide impact.  It addresses this specific accounting failure; it
is not a claim to fix unrelated causes of lockups or RCU stalls.

The affected production machine uses two AMD EPYC 7643 48-Core Processors,
for 96 physical CPU cores in total.  Before the scheduler patches it exhibited
the failures described above; with the patched kernel it has operated stably
without those scheduler crashes and lockups.  This is the observed result on
that system rather than a general guarantee for unrelated hardware or failure
paths.

The scheduler series additionally prevents try_steal_cookie() from migrating
an rq->donor task that is still the active proxy-execution context.

Intel Xe stability
------------------

The Xe changes are kept in three reviewable groups:

* TTM and Xe buffer-object eviction, execution recovery, GuC register, and
  page-table error-path fixes.
* D3Cold is disabled by default for Battlemage devices to retain the stable
  power-management behavior previously obtained through power/control=on.
* Additional upstream fixes for the GuC steered-register allocation,
  BO-prefetch bounds, imported dma-buf lifetime, LNL/Battlemage WOPCM sizing,
  page-table retry state, scheduler timeline-name lifetime, and GT resume
  failures.

The additional Xe group carries upstream commits 24f60b8e9f44,
632ecc90e1ca, 7bc597ce74ba, 62775525a27c, ad87e2476b3b, 6384271ac1ac,
299bc6d50b1b, and 644673a69f2b, adapted to the Linux 6.18 interfaces without
importing later multi-queue interfaces.

The GuC allocation fix uses bitmap_or() followed by bitmap_weight() because
the combined bitmap_weighted_or() helper was introduced after the Linux 6.18
branch.  Commit 8a0fb57675be is intentionally not carried: it fixes purged-BO
state handling introduced by post-6.18 commit ad9843aac91a, so its prerequisite
and affected behavior do not exist in Linux 6.18.

Other changes and configuration
-------------------------------

The branch also carries the selected builddeb, Cachy, crypto, and NTFS-Plus
changes.  NTFS-Plus is imported from namjaejeon/linux-ntfs commit
ca149db6b901a0d44cdd88e54047e0cd422ca70d.

The exact build configuration is stored as:

	arch/x86/configs/datiscum_defconfig

Important settings include CONFIG_DRM_XE=m, CONFIG_NTFS_FS=m,
CONFIG_NTFS_FS_POSIX_ACL=y, CONFIG_NTFS3_FS=m, and the local version suffix
"-datiscum1".

Production tracing and debugging profile
----------------------------------------

The production configuration uses the ORC unwinder instead of frame pointers.
The x86 Kconfig documentation estimates a 5-10 percent overall performance
improvement for ORC compared with the frame-pointer unwinder.  ORC retains
reliable kernel stack traces while using a few megabytes of additional runtime
memory.

The NMI/perf hard-lockup detector and the scheduler stack-end check are
disabled.  The soft-lockup detector and hung-task detector remain enabled so
that future scheduler or driver regressions can still be reported.

General tracepoints, dynamic Ftrace, function tracing, Kprobes, Uprobes, BPF,
BPF JIT, BTF, Perf, and KVM remain enabled.  The configuration deliberately
does not trade away these observability and virtualization interfaces.

The PARANOID_AVG scheduler feature is compiled in for targeted diagnosis but
defaults to disabled.  KFENCE is also compiled in but has a sampling interval
of zero, so periodic KFENCE allocation sampling is disabled.  Memory
initialization on allocation remains enabled as security hardening for the
container host.

No additional kernel command-line option is required for this profile.
In particular, do not add ``nmi_watchdog=1``.  An existing ``nmi_watchdog=1``,
``delayacct``, ``slub_debug``, ``page_owner=on``, or tracing/debug boot option
should be removed unless it is intentionally needed for a new investigation.

For an older running kernel that still contains the hard-lockup detector, its
runtime overhead can be stopped until reboot with:

	sysctl -w kernel.nmi_watchdog=0

The soft-lockup and hung-task detectors should remain active.  Do not set
``kernel.ftrace_enabled=0`` because that would also remove tracing facilities
kept for BPF and targeted diagnostics.  During normal operation,
``current_tracer`` should be ``nop``, and the scheduler feature list should
contain ``NO_PARANOID_AVG``:

	cat /sys/kernel/tracing/current_tracer
	grep -o 'NO_PARANOID_AVG\|PARANOID_AVG' \
		/sys/kernel/debug/sched/features

If PARANOID_AVG was enabled manually, disable it with:

	echo NO_PARANOID_AVG > /sys/kernel/debug/sched/features

Validation
----------

The complete patch series was applied without rejects to a clean v6.18.40
tree.  The resulting source files were compared byte-for-byte with the
previously prepared local Linux 6.18.40 tree.  The only subsequent source
cleanup removed two trailing blank lines reported by git diff --check.  No
kernel compilation was run as part of this repository import.
